{"title":"How plants make their own food using sunlight","key_idea":"Plants use sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to make glucose (food) and release oxygen.","method":["Identify the three things a plant needs: sunlight from the sun, water from the soil, and carbon dioxide from the air.","Understand that the plant uses the sun's energy to turn these three things into glucose, which is the food the plant needs to grow.","Remember that oxygen is released into the air as a leftover product when the plant makes its food."],"worked_example":{"problem":"A potted plant sits on a sunny windowsill with water in its soil. What does the plant use to make food?","steps":["The plant gets sunlight through the window.","The plant's roots take up water from the soil.","The plant's leaves take in carbon dioxide from the air around it.","The plant combines all three to make glucose for growth and releases oxygen."],"answer":"The plant uses sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide to make glucose."},"key_terms":[{"term":"Photosynthesis","meaning":"the process plants use to make food from sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide"},{"term":"Glucose","meaning":"the sugar food that plants make and use to grow"},{"term":"Carbon dioxide","meaning":"a gas in the air that plants need to make food"}],"common_mistakes":["Thinking plants get food from the soil like animals eat from plates; actually plants make their own food.","Forgetting that plants need sunlight and thinking water alone is enough."],"remember":"Sunlight + water + carbon dioxide = glucose + oxygen.","quick_check":[{"q":"What are the three things a plant needs to make food?","a":"Sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide."},{"q":"What does a plant release into the air when it makes food?","a":"Oxygen."}],"tell_your_child":"Think of your plant like a tiny factory: it takes in three things and turns them into food so it can grow tall and strong. That is why plants love sunny spots!"}
